ar transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-38905/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-38905"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-141461/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "caeb2f46-e599-554b-8c3e-d0c29cbcbc2f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141461/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "64c0bc92-7099-51f5-8533-1ec0b72af270" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141461/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "e2661115-9e68-5340-9609-eb68917633d6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141461/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "cd9a48c0-67a0-5d20-8d27-7fb996eb2b4d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db