trix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0},$ which means there is no non-zero vector $v$ such that $T(v)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-141461/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141461"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-141460/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "2c8440cd-e694-508d-b694-7db873e958fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141460/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "f33032ef-3d2d-531f-a37e-a9081928e853" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141460/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "ae06088a-a465-52c4-949b-be84f63f0258" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141460/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "07377e4f-6501-59e8-a4ea-b097fb45031a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141460/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "15308645-3e58-5493-aa09-8622b720ea4e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141460/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f25cb538-21d5-54a4-9e90-f2162a989ad4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-141460/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-141460/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-141460/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-141460/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-141460/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-141460/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-141460" :question/id #uuid "e6231d52-ba27-5624-97d0-175d96fdf993" :question/math-academy-id "q-141460" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(x)=v$ has no solution for some vector $v?$ \n\n- $T$ is not invertible\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-field